  {:title
   "doc->ingestion-run decodes :failure/ref via keyword — violates the record's own schema and corrupts slashy ref names on export→import round-trip",
   :file "src/epiphany/infra/adapters/mongo.clj",
   :detail
   "mongo.clj:284: (assoc :failure/ref (keyword (.getString f \"ref\"))). git/ref-name is a string schema (law/git.clj:27 [:re ref-name-pattern]); real values are strings like \"refs/heads/main\" (infra/git.clj:78, test/epiphany/infra/git_commit_test.clj:181). Decoding to a keyword makes the decoded record fail its own contract, and re-encoding on import-all corrupts the data: (name (keyword \"refs/heads/main\")) => \"main\" — the export-all → import-all → export-all path (which this commit made callable for the first time via the .into fix, and which ENG-021A backup/restore will depend on) silently loses the \"refs/heads/\" prefix. Same pre-existing vintage as the failure/oid bug; 2573e7e's decode-fix pass missed it.",
   :severity "should-fix",
   :card "eng-017e",
   :lens "correctness",
   :survives false,
   :reasons
   ["The mechanics are real — mongo.clj:284 decodes :failure/ref via (keyword ...), the schema (law/observation.clj:149 -> git/ref-name, a string [:re] in law/git.clj:27) requires a string, producers store strings (infra/git.clj:78,116), and (name (keyword \"refs/heads/main\")) => \"main\" would corrupt on re-encode. But this is not a finding against THIS card. (1) Grandfathered code: git blame shows line 284 dates to 0a99597d (ENG-001A era, 2026-07-12); the card's commit 91ab60f never touched the ingestion-run decoder, and the claim itself concedes 'pre-existing vintage' missed by 2573e7e (a different card's fix pass). (2) Explicitly out of scope: the card's Non-goals state 'No decode/read-path validation (ENG-017F). No BSON schema redesign; existing encoding stays unless a law failure proves it wrong' — decode fidelity is ENG-017F's assigned work, and no law failure proved it wrong because the law suite's ingestion-run fixture uses :ingestion/failures [] (observations_laws.clj:88), so the field is never exercised. (3) No delivered behavior is broken: the card's full-map decode idempotency equality applies only to repository-location (insert-or-idempotent!, mongo.clj:579-600); record-ingestion-run! uses validated-insert! where duplicate-key replays return nil without decoding, so no false :idempotency-conflict arises. Write-path validation passes because incoming records carry string refs. (4) The claim's only hook — that the .into fix made export-all callable — doesn't transfer ownership: the .into fix was a correct, complete side-fix so the law suite could snapshot state; latent decode bugs in exported records (and the export→import round-trip) belong to ENG-017F and the already-delivered ENG-021A backup/restore drill, neither of which this card's acceptance criteria cover. Real bug, wrong card — route it to ENG-017F rather than gating ENG-017E's review→done promotion."
    "Verified every link of the claim against the code. mongo.clj:284 does decode :failure/ref via (keyword (.getString f \"ref\")), while the law schema (law/observation.clj:149 → law/git.clj:27) defines git/ref-name as [:re ref-name-pattern] — a string-only schema requiring '^(HEAD|refs/[^ ]+)$'. Producers confirm strings: infra/git.clj:78 and :116 put the raw ref-name string, and git_commit_test.clj:181/188 assert (= \"refs/heads/nonexistent\" (:failure/ref ...)). The decoded record therefore fails its own contract (a keyword never satisfies [:re]). The corruption claim is also exactly right: ingestion-run->doc (mongo.clj:254-255) re-encodes with (name ref), and (name (keyword \"refs/heads/main\")) => \"main\", silently dropping the 'refs/heads/' prefix on the export-all → import-all path (mongo.clj:717-766, where import-all calls ingestion-run->doc directly with no validation to catch it). Scope refutation fails: the card's ENG-017F non-goal covers read-path *validation*, not decode correctness, and this very card fixed the pre-existing export-all .into bug — making this round-trip callable for the first time, as the card's own completion comment discloses ('No caller existed before the law suite; backup/restore (ENG-021A) would have hit this'). Furthermore, commit 2573e7e in this same review cycle fixed three sibling Mongo decode bugs in this exact function (getList removal, oid-as-UUID, empty request-id) and simply missed this fourth instance of the identical bug class — it is not untouchable grandfathered code but a residual miss in an actively-fixed area. No card comment documents it as an accepted tradeoff. Silent corruption of Git-canonical ref names on a backup/restore-relevant path directly contradicts the repo's evidence-preservation domain rule, and the fix is one token (drop `keyword`). Real, in-scope, promotion-gating."]}
  {:title
   "Scope's encode-cli not delivered as a shared function — four CLI handlers inline their own category->exit mapping",
   :file "src/epiphany/infra/main.clj",
   :detail
   "Scope bullet: 'encode-cli / encode-http ... one source of truth, not two parallel maps.' The HTTP side has the single shared table (application/commands.clj http-status-for-category L145-155 + http.clj encode-outcome L215-228), but the CLI side has four inline copies of the category->exit-code mapping with divergent detail formatting: run-register L137-146 (appends 'Code: unavailable'), run-search L405-416 (code + Ollama hint), run-inbox-decide L1142-1147 (code only), run-status L232-235 (detail only). All ACs are met as written and exit-code parity (accepted=0, else 1) holds and is regression-tested, so this is the residual Scope-vs-delivery gap, not an AC failure — but the 'one source of truth' claim currently holds only for the HTTP half.",
   :severity "nit",
   :card "eng-017g2",
   :lens "dod-coverage",
   :survives false,
   :reasons
   ["The code reading is accurate — main.clj's run-register/run-search/run-status/run-inbox-decide each inline `(if (= :accepted category) {:exit 0 ...} {:exit 1 ...})` rather than calling a shared encode-cli, and application/commands.clj only exposes http-status-for-category/http-title-for-category (L145-155). But this does not survive as a promotion-gating finding: (1) the CLI 'mapping' is a trivial binary (accepted=0, else 1) with no per-category semantics that could diverge the way an HTTP status table can — the only semantic content is the outcome category itself, which IS shared via commands/execute; the varying suffixes ('Code: unavailable', Ollama hint) are surface-appropriate UX text, not a parallel category map. (2) The claim itself concedes all four ACs are met as written, exit-code parity holds, and parity is regression-tested (test/epiphany/parity/cli_http_test.clj extended to status + review-decisions per the IMPLEMENTED comment), and self-classifies severity as 'nit'. (3) The card's delivered comment (L107) honestly discloses exactly what was built: 'encode tables (single HTTP category->status map)' — no concealment, and the ACs never required an encode-cli symbol, only routing through decode→execute→encode, which the handlers demonstrably do (decode at L95/231/401, execute, then encode-as-{:exit :out}). The residual Scope-bullet-vs-delivery nuance is legitimate follow-up/refactor material for a future card, but a self-described nit with green ACs and tested parity does not gate a review→done promotion."
    "The claim is factually accurate about the code shape — verified main.clj L137-146 (run-register), L405-416 (run-search), L1142-1147 (run-inbox-decide), and L232-235 (run-status) each inline their own category->exit handling, while only the HTTP side has the shared table (application/commands.clj http-status-for-category L145-149 consumed by http.clj encode-outcome L215+). But it does not survive as a promotion-gating finding: (1) The reviewer self-classifies it as a nit and concedes all four ACs are met and exit-code parity holds and is regression-tested. (2) The CLI 'mapping' is degenerate — accepted=0, everything else=1, identical in all four sites — so there is no actual parallel-map divergence risk against the HTTP table; what varies between the four sites is human-facing detail text (e.g. the Ollama hint on search), which is legitimately command-specific and would not belong in a single shared formatter anyway. (3) The parity suite (extended to status and review-decisions per the card's IMPLEMENTED comment) structurally guards the only cross-surface contract that matters (0/1 exit vs 200/400/404/503), so the residual gap is cosmetic code-duplication inside one surface, not a parity hazard. A nit that fails no AC and carries no divergence risk is not worth gating a review->done promotion; it can be filed as a follow-up refactor if anyone cares."]}
  {:title
