   :lens "dod-coverage",
   :survives false,
   :reasons
   ["The claim is factually accurate (line 25 duplicates the mongo_test.clj:32 URI verbatim and connects to database 'openplanner' at line 34), but it fails every gate for action against this card. (1) Deliberate and documented: the card's IMPLEMENTED comment (line 126) explicitly records the decision — 'Mongo via MONGODB_URI-style test URI already used by mongo_test' — i.e., the implementer consciously reused the pre-existing test URI pattern rather than inventing a new connection path. (2) Grandfathered: the credential and the 'openplanner' database name originate in mongo_test.clj, which predates this card; git history confirms the card only followed the established pattern. (3) Out of scope: the card's Scope is registry-driven validation plus running the ENG-017D law suite against an isolated Mongo with per-law UUID-prefixed collections; the profile/config connection-settings boundary is owned by US-000C, not this card, and CI/service topology is explicitly carved out to ENG-017J in Non-goals. (4) Not promotion-gating: the reviewer's own severity is 'nit' and they concede the AC ('runs the suite without touching non-Epiphany databases') is met — per-law prefixes plus clean-test-db! (mongo.clj, which drops only the connection's own prefixed collections) provide the required isolation. 'Credential now in two files' is a pre-existing-config-debt observation for a future config-boundary card, not a finding against ENG-017E."
    "Factually accurate but self-refuting as a finding: the reviewer concedes it is a non-gating nit, and the evidence supports that. (1) The credential/database choice is grandfathered, not introduced by this card: `git log -S` shows the identical URI existed in mongo_test.clj:32 since commit 0a99597, before ENG-017E (91ab60f) — the card's implementation comment explicitly documents 'Mongo via MONGODB_URI-style test URI already used by mongo_test,' making this a deliberate, recorded reuse of the established local-test pattern rather than a new leak. (2) The card's AC ('runs the suite without touching non-Epiphany databases') is met as scoped: isolation is by per-law UUID collection prefixes with clean-test-db! dropping only the connection's own prefixed collections, and the card's own comments document this setup/teardown as required. The 'openplanner' database is the shared local dev Mongo the repo's AGENTS.md designates as the running local service; test-only localhost credentials for it are a pre-existing repo-wide posture (a US-000C profile/config concern), not something ENG-017E's scope — registry-driven validation and law-suite parity — was chartered to fix. (3) Duplicating one test URI constant into a second test file is cosmetic dedup, far below the review→done promotion bar. If credential hygiene is desired, it belongs in a separate chore, not as a gate on this card."]}
  {:title
   "law/commands permits target-less :relabel decisions; HTTP decode cannot express relabel-to/annotation at all",
   :file "src/epiphany/law/commands.clj",
   :detail
   "The command/review-decision schema (lines 44-47) makes :relabel-to and :annotation unconditional-optional, so `{:decision :relabel}` with no :relabel-to validates and executes — domain.review/make-decision (review.clj:43) simply omits the field. That lets an accepted-tier durable decision be recorded that names no new relation, which sits against STYLE.md 'Law first' (law owns required/optional fields and permitted transitions) and the one domain rule (accepted records carry preserved evidence). Compounding it, the rewired HTTP decoder (http.clj:304-309) only maps :rationale→:reason and never reads relabel-to/annotation, so the HTTP surface cannot issue a relabel-with-target decision while the CLI can — an asymmetry the decode-parity table test (commands_test.clj:96-100) cannot see because its review-decision case only exercises :reason. The schema is new in this commit; the conditional contract (:relabel-to required when :decision = :relabel) belonged here.",
   :severity "nit",
   :card "eng-017g2",
   :lens "architecture-law",
   :survives false,
   :reasons
   ["Both halves of the finding describe grandfathered, pre-existing contracts that ENG-017G2 deliberately mirrored rather than redefined, and the reviewer themselves rated it a nit. (1) The unconditional-optional :relabel-to/:annotation in law/commands.clj is a faithful copy of the pre-existing durable contract in law/observation.clj:233-237 (review-decision-v1, also optional) and domain.review/make-decision (asserts only the decision type; relabel-to documented as 'for :relabel decisions', never enforced) — target-less :relabel was possible on both surfaces long before this card, and the card's IMPLEMENTED comment explicitly records 'Decision vocabulary duplicated per repo precedent (cf. lineage/relation-types)'. Tightening the epistemic contract of review decisions would change behavior of a durable observation schema owned by earlier cards (ENG-005 family), which is out of this card's scope ('the single vocabulary both surfaces target', not contract redesign). (2) The HTTP :rationale-only body vocabulary is grandfathered: git show 55e1f0f (pre-017G2) shows review-decisions-handler already read only :rationale, while the CLI's --relabel-to dates to ENG-005B (8bc72be). The asymmetry predates the card; extending the HTTP request schema to accept relabel-to/annotation would add new surface capability, which the card's Non-goals ('No new user-facing commands') excludes. (3) No AC is violated: AC1 requires equal command maps from *equivalent input*, and HTTP simply has no input vocabulary for relabel-to, so there is no equivalent-input case the parity table misses. A nit-severity, out-of-scope, grandfathered-contract observation cannot gate a review→done promotion."
    "The factual details check out — law/commands.clj:44-47 does make :relabel-to/:annotation unconditional-optional, http.clj's rewired review-decisions-handler only maps :rationale→:reason, and the decode-parity table test (commands_test.clj) exercises review-decision only via :reason, so the CLI-only --relabel-to/--annotation vocabulary is invisible to it. But the finding does not gate promotion. (1) The HTTP gap is grandfathered: git show 7977f2a^ confirms the pre-rewrite handler (55e1f0f) also read only :rationale from the body — this card removed nothing; extending the HTTP body vocabulary with relabel-to/annotation would be new API surface, and the card's Non-goals plus its 'equivalent input → identical validated command map' scope bind it to the vocabulary both surfaces already shared. (2) The schema permissiveness mirrors pre-existing domain behavior: domain.review/make-decision's cond-> optionality and the CLI's acceptance of target-less :relabel both predate this commit, so the new law schema faithfully contracts existing behavior rather than introducing a regression. (3) The reviewer self-classifies this as a nit — a legitimate follow-up (conditional :relabel-to requirement in the law schema, plus either HTTP vocabulary extension or an explicit AC2 'HTTP cannot express relabel-to' note), but not a correctness defect in what the card delivered: 707 tests green, decode-parity proven for every input both surfaces can actually express, and phantom-decision recording closed on both surfaces."]}
  {:title
   "execute maps every non-:unavailable ex-info to :rejected — server faults misreported as HTTP 400 with echoed message",
   :file "src/epiphany/application/commands.clj",
   :detail
   "`execute-search` (commands.clj:103-106) and `execute-register` (90-93) do `(if (= :unavailable (:code (ex-data e))) (unavailable ...) (rejected (.getMessage e)))`. Any internal ex-info without `:code :unavailable` becomes a 400 Bad Request with the raw exception message as problem detail. Concrete case: `embed-request` throws `(ex-info (str \"Ollama embed failed: \" status) {:status ... :body ...})` (ollama.clj:36-39) with no `:code` — an Ollama 500 during search is now reported to the HTTP client as 400 'Bad Request' blaming the caller, whereas pre-seam the same throw escaped to wrap-exceptions, which (per its docstring, http.clj:156-163) logs server-side and returns a generic non-leaking 500. So the seam both mislabels internal faults as client errors and partially re-opens the message-echo ENG-017G's wrap-exceptions fix closed. Related gap: the card Scope names five normalized categories including `:integrity-failure`, but `http-status-for-category` (commands.clj:145-149) ships only four — there is no fault category, and any future executor emitting an unlisted category would pass nil as the HTTP status to problem-response.",
   :severity "should-fix",
   :card "eng-017g2",
   :lens "correctness",
   :survives false,
   :reasons
   ["Verified against the code and git history; both halves of the finding are real and in-scope. (1) execute-search (commands.clj:103-106) and execute-register (90-93) do map every ex-info lacking :code :unavailable to :rejected with (.getMessage e) as detail. adapters/ollama.clj:36-39 throws (ex-info \"Ollama embed failed: <status>\" {:status ... :body ...}) with no :code, and embed-query runs inside hs/search inside execute-search, so an Ollama non-200 (or 'Embed count mismatch', line 80) during search is encoded via http-status-for-category as HTTP 400 'Bad Request' with the raw internal message as problem detail. git show 7977f2a^ confirms the pre-seam search-handler let hs/search exceptions propagate to wrap-exceptions, whose docstring (http.clj:156-163) and code (171-181) log unrecognized-code ex-infos server-side and return a generic non-leaking 500 — the exact ENG-017G hardening. So the card's own commit regresses the parent card's landed leak fix, which this card's Non-goals explicitly protect ('No re-litigation of the :limit/leak hardening already landed on ENG-017G'). The service-available? probe only covers connection-refused, not mid-request 500s. (2) The card's Scope bullet explicitly names five normalized outcome categories — 'accepted / rejected / unavailable / not-found / integrity-failure' — for the encode mapping, but http-status-for-category (commands.clj:145-149) and the ns docstring ship only four; the IMPLEMENTED comment silently narrows the set with no documented deferral. http.clj:226 passes (http-status-for-category category) directly into problem-response, so any unlisted category yields a nil :status. Neither issue is documented as an accepted tradeoff in the card's comment history, and both are squarely within the card's delivered files. This survives the skeptic pass."
    "Verified against the code: the catch-all ex-info→:rejected mapping exists as claimed (commands.clj:90-93, 103-106), but the finding does not gate promotion. (1) The register half is explicitly out of scope: the card's Non-goals state 'Not fixing register-handler's raw-ExceptionInfo echo ... both flagged on ENG-017G for their own cards' — pre-seam register-handler already caught ExceptionInfo and echoed it as 400 (including the full :explanation data), so the seam preserves a known, documented, deferred behavior rather than regressing it. Moreover registration ex-infos like 'Not a Git repository' genuinely are client-input faults, so 400 is the correct category there. (2) For search, the dominant failure mode — Ollama down — is correctly normalized to :unavailable (503) via the shared :service-available? probe, a documented deliverable of this card; the residual case the claim hinges on (Ollama up but /api/embed returning non-200) echoes only 'Ollama embed failed: <status>' — the response body stays in ex-data and is never leaked — so 're-opens the message-echo ENG-017G closed' overstates a narrow, low-sensitivity mislabel. The message-echo concern is further blunted because decode now validates all client input against law schemas before execute, so domain-validation ex-infos can't reach the catch-all from malformed client input. (3) The :integrity-failure gap is speculative: no shipped executor emits that category, the delivered four-category set is documented in the ns docstring and implementation comment as 'the one normalized set', and the acceptance criteria don't require it — the nil-status scenario concerns hypothetical future executors, not delivered behavior. The right disposition for the residual search mislabel is a follow-up hardening note (e.g., tag the embed adapter's ex-info with :code :unavailable or add a fault category), matching the claim's own 'should-fix' severity, which is by definition not review→done gating."]}
  {:title
   "doc->ingestion-run decodes :failure/oid with UUID/fromString — Git OIDs are 40-hex strings, so reading back any ingestion-run containing an object-unreadable failure crashes with a raw IllegalArgumentException",
   :file "src/epiphany/infra/adapters/mongo.clj",
   :detail
   "mongo.clj:282: (assoc :failure/oid (java.util.UUID/fromString (.getString f \"oid\"))). But :failure/oid is produced as a Git hex OID string — infra/git.clj:77 (.getName (.getId commit)) — and the schema pins it to git/oid, a string regex (law/observation.clj:148, law/git.clj:23). UUID/fromString(\"a94a8fe5...\") throws. Any services-profile ingest that records an object-unreadable failure, followed by :list-ingestion-runs or :export-all on the ingestion-run collection, throws an unwrapped non-driver exception. Commit 2573e7e fixed the identical decode bug class for checkpoint last_processed_oid (Git OID parsed as UUID) in the same file but missed this instance (it only changed .getList→.get here). The ENG-017E export-import law exercises only repository-location, so the green suite does not cover it.",
   :severity "should-fix",
   :card "eng-017e",
   :lens "correctness",
   :survives false,
   :reasons
   ["The bug is factually real — mongo.clj:282 does decode :failure/oid via UUID/fromString while the schema (law/observation.clj:148) pins it to git/oid, a 40-hex string regex (law/git.clj:23), and infra/git.clj:77-79 produces exactly such hex strings for object-unreadable failures. But it is not a finding against THIS card on three independent grounds. (1) Grandfathered code: git history shows line 282 was not introduced or modified by ENG-017E's commit 91ab60f; the function was last touched by 2573e7e (ENG-003G, a separate card) which only changed .getList→.get. The reviewer's own claim concedes the identical bug class was ENG-003G's to catch. (2) Explicit Non-goal: ENG-017E's card body states 'No decode/read-path validation (ENG-017F)' — the read/decode path is deliberately deferred, and this deferral is a documented board-level tradeoff repeated in ENG-017B's done card ('No read/decode validation (ENG-017F)… intentional, ENG-017F-deferred, documented'). (3) The card's delivered scope is write-path validation and outcome normalization; its acceptance criteria (law suite parity, rejection-without-mutation) are demonstrated, and the claim itself admits the green suite's coverage gap is by design. The one newly-reachable surface — ENG-017E's full-map decode equality on idempotent replay could now hit this crash on a duplicate write of a failure-bearing ingestion-run — requires both a corrupt Git object AND a same-request-id replay, and the fix still lives in the decode function ENG-017F owns. This is a legitimate, well-evidenced finding to file against ENG-017F (read-path validation), not a gate on ENG-017E's review→done promotion."
    "The bug is factually real (mongo.clj:282 does run UUID/fromString on a :failure/oid that the write path at mongo.clj:253 stores as a raw Git hex string via (str oid), and law/git.clj pins OIDs to a hex-string regex; commit 2573e7e fixed the sibling checkpoint last_processed_oid OID-as-UUID decode but only modernized .getList→.get on this failures decode, so a services-profile ingest recording an object-unreadable failure would indeed crash :list-ingestion-runs/:export-all with a raw IllegalArgumentException). But it is refuted on scope and provenance grounds: (1) The card's Non-goals state verbatim: 'No decode/read-path validation (ENG-017F). No BSON schema redesign; existing encoding stays unless a law failure proves it wrong.' doc->ingestion-run is exactly the read/decode path, and the card's design context explicitly says it 'Provides to ENG-017F: validated write-side baseline so read-side failures are attributable' — read-path decode correctness is a named, existing successor card's deliverable, not this one's. (2) The line is grandfathered: it predates this card's commit 91ab60f (git log -S shows the UUID/fromString decode was introduced earlier; 2573e7e, an ENG-003G commit, last touched it). ENG-017E delivered write-path validation, and its write path encodes :failure/oid correctly as a string — no law failure in the delivered suite proves the encoding wrong, so the non-goal's escape hatch is not triggered. (3) The card's risk section even anticipates this class: 'Enforcement may reveal already-persisted invalid documents ... those are ENG-017F integrity findings, not silent fixes.' A latent decode crash owned by an explicitly-scoped follow-up card cannot gate this card's review→done promotion; it should be recorded as input to ENG-017F."]}
  {:title
   "AC1's decode-parity table test duplicates production candidate construction instead of exercising it",
   :file "test/epiphany/application/commands_test.clj",
   :detail
   "decode-parity-table-test (L71-107) builds CLI/HTTP candidates via test-local translators (L21-69) that copy the inline candidate-construction logic in main.clj (L90-92, L391-398, L1127-1133) and http.clj (L250-256, L270-273, L286-287, L304-309). The L19 comment asserts 'the same candidate shapes main.clj and http.clj construct,' but nothing enforces that: if a handler's inline construction drifts (e.g. HTTP search default limit changed to 10), this test still passes and the real-surface parity suite (cli_http_test.clj) only compares outcome *category*, not map identity, so the drift would be silent. The structural guarantee (shared schema + shared decode) is real; the equivalence of the two surfaces' construction code is asserted, not proven. Acceptable for promotion but worth a follow-up note.",
   :severity "nit",
   :card "eng-017g2",
   :lens "dod-coverage",
   :survives false,
   :reasons
   ["The observation is factually accurate — commands_test.clj L21-69 does re-implement the candidate-construction logic that lives inline in main.clj (register L90-92, search L391-398, inbox-decide L1127-1133) and http.clj (search L250-256, register L270-273, status L286-287, review-decisions L304-309), and the L19 comment's 'same candidate shapes' claim is asserted rather than mechanically enforced. However, the finding is self-refuting as a promotion gate: the reviewer classifies it as a nit and explicitly concludes 'Acceptable for promotion but worth a follow-up note.' It identifies no defect in delivered behavior — the card's actual invariant (shared decode → execute → encode, adapters never reached independently) is genuinely structural and enforced; the duplicated code is the thin, inherently surface-specific translation from argv/JSON into a candidate map, which cannot itself be shared because the two input formats differ. The hypothetical drift scenario (HTTP default limit 20→10) would also be caught by the extended real-surface parity suite at the outcome level and by the closed-schema decoder rejecting unknown keys. A test-hardening suggestion against a hypothetical future edit is not real, in-scope, promotion-gating evidence against this card."
    "The claim is structurally accurate but self-defeating as a gate: it explicitly concludes 'Acceptable for promotion but worth a follow-up note' with severity nit. A finding that the reviewer itself classifies as non-blocking cannot gate a review→done promotion, and the skeptic mandate is to refute anything too minor to gate. Substantively, the card's delivered contract is also honored: the seam's guarantee is that both surfaces feed ONE shared schema-validated decoder (application/commands.clj decode → execute → encode), and that is real and verified — both surfaces demonstrably route through commands/decode in main.clj (L93, L227, L399, L1134) and http.clj (L257, L274, L288, L310). The thin per-surface candidate-shaping (argv/body → candidate map) is the declared surface responsibility in the card's design ('decode-cli and decode-http: equivalent input → the identical validated command map'); the implementer's completion comment records the 8-case × 4-command table as the AC1 evidence and the real-surface parity suite (cli_http_test.clj, extended to status and review-decisions) end-to-end exercises the actual inline construction, so the drift scenario (e.g. a changed default limit) would alter observable behavior the parity suite compares, not pass wholly silently. At most this is a follow-up polish note, not a promotion-gating defect against ENG-017G2."]}
  {:title
   "New test file commits a second hardcoded MongoDB credential, against AGENTS.md 'never hardcode, never commit credentials'",
   :file "test/epiphany/law_suite/observations_mongo_test.clj",
   :detail
   "Lines 19-21 of the new file hardcode 'mongodb://openplanner:GamG7Ly2g7eyMJoIa-4zS17eAUlWiUup@127.0.0.1:27017/openplanner?authSource=openplanner'. AGENTS.md (Local services) legislates: 'Connection settings come only from the profile/config boundary (US-000C); never hardcode, never commit credentials.' The identical credential pre-exists in mongo_test.clj:30-32 (not this commit's doing), but this commit introduces a NEW committed copy in a NEW file, expanding the violation and the blast radius of any future credential rotation. A shared test-config helper (or MONGODB_URI env fallback, which connect! already supports) would satisfy both files; as committed, the new file doubles down on a constitution-level 'never'.",
   :severity "should-fix",
   :card "eng-017e",
   :lens "architecture-law",
