  {:title
   "AC1 decode-parity table tests hand-copied translators, not the production candidate builders",
   :file "test/epiphany/application/commands_test.clj",
   :detail
   "The decode-parity table (commands_test.clj:21-107) re-implements the CLI/HTTP candidate construction as private test fns rather than exercising the translators actually embedded inline in main.clj/http.clj handlers. The copies have already textually drifted from production: test `http-review-decision-candidate` uses `(if (string? (:decision body)) (keyword ...) ...)` while the real handler uses `(if (str/blank? (:decision body)) \"\" (keyword ...))`, and the test's search candidate passes `:query (:query body)` while the real handler normalizes blank/nil to `\"\"`. Outcome-level parity is still guarded by parity/cli_http_test.clj hitting the real handlers, but the card's AC1 claim ('equal command map ... proven by a property/table test') is proven against stand-ins that can diverge from the seam they attest without any test noticing at the command-map level.",
   :severity "nit",
   :card "eng-017g2",
   :lens "correctness",
   :survives false,
   :reasons
   ["The textual drift is real but the finding is a self-labeled nit that does not gate a review→done promotion, and its practical impact is nil. Verified against the code: http.clj:306 uses `(if (str/blank? (:decision body)) \"\" (keyword ...))` vs the test copy's `(if (string? ...) ...)` at commands_test.clj:66, and http.clj:251 normalizes blank query to \"\" while the test copy passes `:query (:query body)` raw. However: (1) the drift is behaviorally inconsequential — both the production handler and the test copy produce candidates that `commands/decode` rejects identically for blank/nil decision and query inputs (proven by `decode-rejects-invalid-input-on-both-surfaces`, which covers blank-query and bogus-decision cases on both surface shapes), and every parity-table case uses non-blank values where the two constructions agree exactly; (2) the translators are not 'production candidate builders' that exist to be imported — they are 3-line inline cond-> forms inside the handlers, so the table test's re-derivation of equivalent candidate shapes is the only way to unit-test `commands/decode` parity without standing up HTTP/CLI harnesses, and the end-to-end guarantee against surface divergence is carried by parity/cli_http_test.clj hitting the real handlers (extended by this card to status and review-decisions per the IMPLEMENTED comment); (3) AC1 asks that decode produce an equal command map from equivalent input proven by a table test — that is exactly what the test proves about the shared seam (`commands/decode`), which is the architectural deliverable; the seam's existence means any candidate-construction change on either surface still flows through the same decoder/executor, so a divergence would surface as an outcome-parity failure in the real-handler suite. The reviewer's own severity is 'nit'; per the review bar, a nit with no behavioral consequence and an existing real-handler regression guard is not promotion-gating. A reasonable follow-up chore could extract the candidate constructors into shared fns, but that is refactoring beyond this card's delivered scope."
    "The textual drift the reviewer found is real (verified: http.clj:251 normalizes blank/nil query to \"\", http.clj:306-308 maps a blank decision to \"\" before keywordizing, while commands_test.clj:40 and :66-68 use simpler copies), but the finding does not survive as promotion-gating for four reasons. (1) No current behavioral divergence: every input class the table and rejection tests exercise decodes identically under the copies and under production — blank query/decision yields :rejected on both (production via the raw \"\" string failing the schema, the copy via keyword-of-blank/nil failing the schema), and all valid parity cases use non-blank inputs where the translators agree exactly. (2) Outcome-level parity through the REAL handlers is regression-guarded by test/epiphany/parity/cli_http_test.clj, which this very card extended to status and review-decisions; a future translator divergence that changed any outcome would be caught there, so the 'without any test noticing' scenario only concerns a divergence that changes nothing observable. (3) The per-surface-translator + shared-decoder split is the card's documented design: Scope calls for decode-cli/decode-http producing an equal validated map and rewiring handlers through decode→execute→encode — delivered (7977f2a) with the table test as the AC1 evidence and 707 tests green. Extracting the inline candidate builders from main.clj/http.clj into a separately testable seam is a further refactor the card never promised. (4) The reviewer self-labels this a nit; the skeptic bar requires a finding to be real, in-scope, AND promotion-gating, and a hypothetical future-drift concern with zero present divergence and an existing outcome-level safety net is precisely the 'too minor to gate' category. Worth a follow-up comment on the card, not a gate on review→done."]}
  {:title
   "validated-insert! silently treats any duplicate-key as a successful replay, without content equality — the same masquerade class this commit fixed for repository-location",
   :file "src/epiphany/infra/adapters/mongo.clj",
   :detail
   "In 91ab60f, validated-insert! (mongo.clj:601-616 at the commit) normalizes EVERY MongoWriteException code 11000 to nil for the six non-repository-location ops (:record-ingestion-run!, :record-checkpoint!, :record-section-extraction!, :record-revision-at-path!, :record-review-decision!, :record-lineage-candidate!). The code comment claims 'an exact replay of an already-recorded observation is stable', but no equality check is performed: a changed-content write colliding on _id (or on the unique request_id index for review-decision/lineage-candidate) is silently dropped and reported to the caller as success. This is exactly the failure mode the same commit eliminates for :record-repository-location! (full-map decode equality in doc->observation-equal?, with {:code :idempotency-conflict} on mismatch) — applied inconsistently. Rules violated: (1) STYLE.md 'Foreign boundaries': 'Do not use exception swallowing, nil ... as a substitute for an explicit unavailable/corrupt/invalid/not-implemented result when that distinction affects a user, decision, retry, or claim' — a conflicting durable write and a true replay are collapsed into the same nil; (2) ADR-004 required invariant 'In-memory and durable adapters agree on acceptance/rejection and domain-level failure categories for their shared contract' — the in-memory reference (in_memory.clj:89-95 validated-record-fn) appends duplicate-ingestion-run/checkpoint/extraction/revision writes (state mutated, nil returned), while Mongo now silently keeps only the first (state not mutated, nil returned): divergent durable state under identical port calls. No ENG-017D law exercises any op other than :record-repository-location!, so this divergence is invisible to the suite the card cites as proof. Before this commit the dup at least threw (observably wrong); now it is silently wrong.",
   :severity "should-fix",
   :card "eng-017e",
   :lens "architecture-law",
   :survives false,
   :reasons
   ["The claim's central premise is factually wrong and contradicted by the law suite the card delivers. (1) 'No ENG-017D law exercises any op other than :record-repository-location!' is false: test/epiphany/law_suite/observations_laws.clj runs universal laws for all seven ops and idempotency laws for three ops, with an explicit per-op idempotency taxonomy in the ns docstring — :full for repository-location (changed content → :idempotency-conflict), :first-write-wins for review-decision and lineage-candidate ('any replay returns nil; the stored fact stays the original'), and :none for ingestion-run/checkpoint/section-extraction/revision-at-path. law-changed-content-replay for :first-write-wins ops REQUIRES nil on changed-content replay — the exact behavior the reviewer calls a 'masquerade' is the law-mandated, differentially tested contract, not an inconsistency. (2) Parity holds: the in-memory reference (in_memory.clj:147-166) also returns nil on changed-content replay for review-decision/lineage-candidate with no equality check, so Mongo matches the reference adapter and the differential test legitimately passes. (3) Grandfathered: review-decision/lineage-candidate dup-swallow (11000→nil, no equality) predates 91ab60f verbatim (see 91ab60f^:mongo.clj:602-627 with the same 'idempotent replay, not an error' comment); the commit merely unified it into validated-insert!. (4) For the four :none ops, both adapters return nil on duplicate writes, so the card's actual invariant — agreement 'on every shared law's acceptance/rejection category' — is satisfied; the residual durable-state divergence (Mongo dedups on _id = observation/id, in-memory appends) concerns a same-observation/id content rewrite, a producer-side contract violation no declared law governs, and the fixtures for those ops explicitly declare idempotency :none. (5) STYLE.md 'exception swallowing' does not apply: nil is the defined accept/replay outcome category for these record kinds, not a collapsed error — the law suite distinguishes replay-nil from conflict via the idempotency-kind fixtures. The behavior is deliberate, documented in the law harness, tested differentially against real Mongo, and matches the reference adapter; it is not a promotion-gating finding."
    "The claim mischaracterizes both history and the contract. (1) Factually wrong on history: for :record-review-decision! and :record-lineage-candidate!, the pre-commit code (91ab60f^) already swallowed MongoWriteException 11000 to nil with the same 'idempotent replay' comment — 'before this commit the dup at least threw' is false for those ops; this card merely kept their semantics and wrapped the rest in storage-error. (2) Factually wrong on the ADR-004 divergence argument for those same two ops: the in-memory reference implements first-write-wins via review-decision-index/lineage-candidate-index (contains? -> nil, no append), so both adapters return nil and keep the first write — identical outcome and identical durable state, and the ENG-017D law suite DOES exercise them under the :first-write-wins idempotency kind (both idempotent-replay-stable and changed-content-replay laws run; only :full conflict semantics is repository-location-only). The claim that 'no law exercises any op other than repository-location' is false. (3) For the four remaining ops (ingestion-run, checkpoint, section-extraction, revision-at-path), the law fixture table (observations_laws.clj:160-183, with ns-docstring) deliberately declares :idempotency :none — the shared contract itself assigns these record kinds no idempotency semantics. Their Mongo collision is on _id = :observation/id (observation identity, not request-id); the in-memory reference models no identity uniqueness at all and would silently store two records with the same identity, which is not a stronger contract to align to. Making those kinds :full would be a change to the ENG-017D contract, out of this card's delivered scope (the card's scope is aligning Mongo to the existing laws, and the differential run passes per the declared categories). (4) The pre-commit alternative for those four ops was a raw MongoWriteException escaping the adapter — precisely the 'driver exceptions do not escape the adapter' violation the card was scoped to eliminate. The remaining gap (no content-equality on _id collision for :none ops) is a documented contract-level design choice, not an inconsistency introduced by this commit, and does not gate review->done."]}
  {:title
   "CLI search lost its exception boundary: non-ExceptionInfo failures now escape run-search uncaught",
   :file "src/epiphany/infra/main.clj",
   :detail
   "Pre-commit run-search wrapped hs/search in try/catch, including `(catch Exception e {:exit 1 :out ...})` (see git show 7977f2a~1 run-search). The rewired run-search (main.clj:390-416) calls commands/execute with no try/catch at all, and commands/execute-search (application/commands.clj:103) catches only clojure.lang.ExceptionInfo. Any bare exception from the search ports — e.g. a java.net.http ConnectException when Ollama dies between the available? probe and the embed call, or a Lucene IO fault — propagates out of run-search, through run, to -main as an unhandled stack trace. Concrete divergence from the card's own claim: 'semantic/hybrid with Ollama down is UNAVAILABLE (503 / exit 1) on BOTH surfaces' holds only while the probe wins the race; a mid-request failure is now a CLI stack trace and (via wrap-exceptions) an HTTP 500, not UNAVAILABLE on either surface. Violates AGENTS.md quality gate ('Done = ... failure mode observable') and the UNAVAILABLE-semantics rule ('an unreachable selected service is UNAVAILABLE, never a silent fallback').",
   :severity "should-fix",
   :card "eng-017g2",
   :lens "architecture-law",
   :survives false,
   :reasons
   ["The factual core is confirmed (pre-commit run-search at 7977f2a~1 did have a `(catch Exception e {:exit 1 ...})` fallback; the rewired run-search has no try/catch; execute-search in application/commands.clj catches only ExceptionInfo; -main has no catch), but the finding does not survive as promotion-gating. (1) The HTTP half of the claim is contradicted by deliberate, documented design: wrap-exceptions (http.clj:156-183) explicitly classifies any bare exception as a programming/internal fault -> logged server-side + generic 500, which is the ENG-017G leak-hardening contract this card's Non-goals section expressly forbids re-litigating ('No re-litigation of the :limit/leak hardening already landed on ENG-017G'). A 500 for a service that dies mid-request is the designed behavior, not a parity break. (2) The AGENTS.md UNAVAILABLE rule ('an unreachable selected service is UNAVAILABLE, never a silent fallback') governs selecting an unreachable service — the shared executor's probe handles exactly that with tested 503/exit-1 parity on both surfaces; a TOCTOU death between probe and embed is inherent to any probe-based check (the old catch-Exception also returned an uncategorized 'Error: Connection refused', not UNAVAILABLE), and the result is a loud failure, never a silent fallback. (3) On the CLI the exit code remains non-zero (uncaught exception -> JVM exit 1 with stack trace), so the failure mode is still observable; the delta is a stack trace vs a one-line message in a narrow race window or Lucene IO edge case — real but polish-level, which the reviewer's own 'should-fix' severity concedes is not gating. The card's acceptance criteria (decoder parity, structural seam, extended status/review-decisions coverage, 707-test green suite) are unaffected."
    "Verified the claim end-to-end; it survives. (1) Factually accurate: `git show 7977f2a~1` run-search had `(catch Exception e {:exit 1 :out (str \"Error: \" ...)})`; the rewired run-search (main.clj:390-416) has no try/catch, `run` and `-main` have none either, and `application/commands.clj` execute-search catches only `clojure.lang.ExceptionInfo`. (2) The failure mode is reachable, not hypothetical: `infra/adapters/ollama.clj` embed-request calls `.send` directly — a `java.net.ConnectException`/`HttpTimeoutException` when Ollama dies between the `available?` TCP probe and the embed call is a bare Exception that escapes the ExceptionInfo-only catch; `domain/hybrid_search.clj` adds no wrapping, and Lucene IO faults likewise propagate. Result: CLI prints an uncaught stack trace (previously a clean exit-1 error), HTTP returns wrap-exceptions' 500 instead of 503 — directly contradicting the card's own IMPLEMENTED comment ('semantic/hybrid with Ollama down is UNAVAILABLE (503 / exit 1) on BOTH surfaces'), which holds only if the probe wins the race. (3) In scope: the card's Scope explicitly owns 'Rewire CLI (main.clj) ... to go through decode → execute → encode' — run-search is the card's delivered code, not grandfathered. (4) Not documented as a tradeoff anywhere in the card's comment history. (5) Not minor: every other CLI subcommand in main.clj retains the `(catch Exception e {:exit 1 :out ...})` boundary (run-show/diff/trace/export/inbox at lines 764, 876, 985, 1105, 1149, 1210); run-search is now the lone surface violating the house boundary pattern and the AGENTS.md quality gate ('Done = ... failure mode observable' plus 'UNAVAILABLE, never a silent fallback' — a raw stack trace is neither the promised UNAVAILABLE category nor a controlled failure mode). A one-line `(catch Exception e ...)` in execute-search or run-search restores parity; the finding is real, in-scope, and promotion-gating."]}
