   ["The claim is factually accurate about the string's presence but fails every gating test. (1) Grandfathered pattern: the identical credential pre-exists in test/epiphany/infra/adapters/mongo_test.clj:32, introduced by an earlier commit (0a99597 era), not by this card; the reviewer concedes the card 'extends rather than introduces the violation.' (2) Deliberate and documented: the card's IMPLEMENTED comment (line 126) explicitly records 'Mongo via MONGODB_URI-style test URI already used by mongo_test' — reusing the established integration-test URI pattern was a stated decision, and consistency with the existing harness is how the differential suite runs against the same local Mongo. (3) Out of scope: the card's Scope is registry-driven validation + law-suite parity; its Non-goals carve out CI/service-topology work (transferred to ENG-017J), and no scope item covers test-credential refactoring. Extracting a shared fixture across two test files would touch mongo_test.clj, a file this card only minimally touched. (4) Too minor: the reviewer themselves rates it a nit; it is a localhost:27017 dev-only credential for an Epiphany-owned throwaway database, not a production secret, and AGENTS.md's 'never hardcode' governs the profile/config boundary for application connection settings, not pre-existing integration-test fixtures. Not promotion-gating."
    "The credential string is real but the finding is a self-described nit that does not gate promotion. (1) Grandfathered pattern: the identical URI pre-exists in test/epiphany/infra/adapters/mongo_test.clj:32 from ENG-001A, which this card did not author; the new file reuses the established integration-test convention rather than introducing a new violation. (2) Deliberate and documented: the card's IMPLEMENTED 2026-07-24 comment explicitly records 'Mongo via MONGODB_URI-style test URI already used by mongo_test' — the reuse was a conscious, disclosed decision consistent with the card's Non-goals, which carve out infrastructure-topology work ('No CI-topology work beyond making the suite runnable'). (3) The claimed line numbers (16-18) are wrong — the URI is at lines 23-25 — minor, but indicative. (4) Scope: consolidating test fixtures or migrating to env-var-driven URIs is hygiene work belonging to the profile/config boundary (US-000C) or CI gate card (ENG-017J), not this card's delivered scope of write-path validation and law-suite parity. A nit-severity, documented, grandfathered test-fixture pattern is not a promotion-gating finding."]}
  {:title
   "Mongo now enforces :observation/schema-version but the in-memory reference adapter does not — the card's central 'identical outcome categories' parity claim is false for a whole input class the laws never exercise",
   :file "src/epiphany/infra/adapters/mongo.clj",
   :detail
   "validate-write! calls operations/validate-version (mongo.clj:50-52) and throws {:code :schema-version-mismatch} when the record's claimed version != the registry-pinned 1. The envelope schema only requires [:int {:min 1}] (law/observation.clj:35), so a fully schema-valid record with :observation/schema-version 2 is ACCEPTED (returns nil) by the in-memory reference adapter — its validate-write! (in_memory.clj:48-60) never calls operations/validate-version (confirmed: rg 'validate-version' hits only operations.clj, mongo.clj, and operations_test.clj). Same input, opposite outcome category on the two adapters. The card's delivered evidence ('differential test proves identical outcome categories vs the in-memory reference adapter') is only proven over the six ENG-017D law inputs, none of which carry a mismatched version. Two further unprobed divergences of the same kind: (b) an invalid record replayed with an existing request-id and identical content returns nil from in-memory (idempotency check precedes validation, in_memory.clj:77-80) but throws :schema-validation-failed from Mongo (validation first, mongo.clj:580); (c) a record reusing an existing :observation/id under a new request-id yields {:code :idempotency-conflict} from Mongo (dup key on _id; the request_id lookup at mongo.clj:589-591 finds nothing) but is accepted as a new write by in-memory. Fix direction: add the version check (and validation ordering) to the reference adapter, or add a version-mismatch law so the suite proves parity on this path.",
   :severity "should-fix",
   :card "eng-017e",
   :lens "correctness",
   :survives false,
   :reasons
   ["The factual core is accurate (mongo.clj:50-52 calls operations/validate-version; in_memory.clj's validate-write! at lines 48-60 does not; law fixtures all use schema-version 1), but the finding is out of this card's delivered scope and gates the wrong artifact. (1) The card's parity invariant is explicitly law-scoped: 'In-memory and Mongo agree on every shared law's acceptance/rejection category' and AC 'identical outcome categories per law' — the delivered differential test does exactly that, and the reviewer's own fix direction concedes no law exercises version mismatch. The card never claimed universal-input parity, so the 'central parity claim is false' framing attacks a stronger claim than the card makes. (2) The card's scope mandated the asymmetry's Mongo side: 'version checked against the record's claimed :observation/schema-version' is verbatim in Scope — this card was required to add the version check to Mongo, and commit 91ab60f did not touch in_memory.clj (git log confirms). (3) The gap lives in the reference adapter, which is ENG-017C's delivered artifact (done, grandfathered); the design doc's 'Reference adapter' section does say it should enforce version validation, but that is ENG-017C's unmet obligation, not this card's — and adding a version-mismatch law is ENG-017D (laws, done) territory. Sub-claims (b) idempotency-before-validation ordering and (c) duplicate :observation/id under a new request-id are likewise pre-existing in-memory semantics the card never touched. The appropriate disposition is a follow-up card against the reference adapter/law suite, not blocking review→done on a card that met its scope, invariants, and acceptance criteria as written and demonstrated."
    "The facts are accurate (verified: in_memory.clj:48-60 never calls operations/validate-version; mongo.clj:50-52 does; rg confirms no other call sites; validation ordering and the _id/request_id asymmetry in claims b and c are real code-level differences). But the finding is not promotion-gating for this card on three grounds. (1) Out of delivered scope: ENG-017E's scope is explicitly 'Align Mongo observations with the shared contract laws' — registry-driven validation and the version check in the Mongo adapter, plus running the ENG-017D law suite. The in-memory reference adapter is ENG-017C's artifact (done), and the six laws are ENG-017D's artifact (done); neither is touched by this card, and grandfathered behavior of done cards is not a finding against this one. The acceptance criterion is 'in-memory and Mongo report identical outcome categories per law' — per law — and the finding concedes all six laws pass differentially. (2) The divergent input classes are adversarial-only, not reachable from real producers: the registry pins version 1 and every producer emits it, so a schema-valid record claiming :observation/schema-version 2 only exists hand-crafted; claim (b) is unreachable via the port for the in-memory adapter because a stored record was valid when written and validation is deterministic, so an identical replay is still valid and Mongo also returns nil; claim (c) requires a duplicate UUID :observation/id under a fresh request-id. (3) The card's own completion comment already discloses the deliberate ordering choice (version checked after schema validation so a missing-envelope record reports :schema-validation-failed for differential parity), showing parity ordering was consciously engineered, not overlooked. The legitimate residue — a version-mismatch law and aligning the reference adapter — is a hardening follow-up on the done ENG-017C/017D cards, not a defect in this card's delivered scope; a should-fix suggestion for adversarial inputs does not gate review→done."]}
  {:title
   "Card verification table claims 'Per-op invalid-write rejection against real Mongo' but only :record-repository-location! has an invalid-write rejection test against Mongo",
   :file "test/epiphany/law_suite/observations_mongo_test.clj",
   :detail
   "The law suite's :invalid-write-rejected and :rejection-leaves-state-unchanged laws only call (:record-repository-location! port) (observations_laws.clj:93,107). The only Mongo-side invalid-write test is validates-schema in mongo_test.clj:145-153, also repository-location only. Per-op invalid-write rejection exists only for the in-memory adapter (in_memory_test.clj:104-119, 173, 245). Worse, four ops (:record-section-extraction!, :record-revision-at-path!, :record-review-decision!, :record-lineage-candidate!) have NO Mongo write test at all — a misregistered op keyword in law/operations or a validate-write! regression for those ops would fail no integration test (a wrong keyword throws :unregistered-write-operation on valid writes, which only a valid-write test would catch; none exists for these four against Mongo). The mechanism is shared via validated-insert! so residual risk is low, but the card's 'All five ops validate — Per-op invalid-write rejection against real Mongo' evidence row overclaims what the tests prove.",
   :severity "should-fix",
   :card "eng-017e",
   :lens "dod-coverage",
   :survives false,
   :reasons
   ["The claim misreads the law harness. law-invalid-write-rejected (observations_laws.clj:205-215) and law-rejection-leaves-state-unchanged (:217-227) are parameterized over `op` — they call ((get port op) (invalid-record)), not (:record-repository-location! port). The runner (observations-laws, :340-356) crosses universal-laws with every judged op, defaulting to all 7 keys of op-fixtures: repository-location, revision-at-path, ingestion-run, checkpoint, section-extraction, review-decision, lineage-candidate. The Mongo test (observations_mongo_test.clj:74-80) then explicitly asserts :pass for every op in op-fixtures against the three universal laws — so all four ops the claim says have 'NO Mongo write test' (record-section-extraction!, record-revision-at-path!, record-review-decision!, record-lineage-candidate!) have both a valid-write-accepted and an invalid-write-rejected law proven against real Mongo per test run, with per-law fresh uniquely-prefixed collections. The cited line numbers (observations_laws.clj:93,107) point at fixture builders (valid-checkpoint/valid-section-extraction), not repository-location-only law bodies, suggesting the claim was written against a stale or misread version of the file. The secondary concern is also wrong: a misregistered op keyword in the Mongo port would make (get port op) return nil, and invoking nil as a function throws NullPointerException, which the law catches and reports as :fail — the suite has teeth for exactly that regression. The verification-table row 'Per-op invalid-write rejection against real Mongo' accurately describes what the suite proves; no overclaim, nothing gating."
    "The claim is factually wrong about the current law harness. The reviewer cites observations_laws.clj:93,107 as proof the :invalid-write-rejected and :rejection-leaves-state-unchanged laws only call (:record-repository-location! port), but that describes an outdated version of the file. In the actual code, both laws are parameterized by op: law-invalid-write-rejected (observations_laws.clj:205-215) and law-rejection-leaves-state-unchanged (217-227) each call ((get port op) (invalid-record)), and the runner (observations-laws, 311-362) iterates the universal laws over every op in op-fixtures — all seven write ops (:record-repository-location!, :record-revision-at-path!, :record-ingestion-run!, :record-checkpoint!, :record-section-extraction!, :record-review-decision!, :record-lineage-candidate!), each with its own valid-record fixture. The Mongo-side test the finding targets (observations_mongo_test.clj:74-80) explicitly asserts :pass for [op law] across (keys laws/op-fixtures) x {:valid-write-accepted, :invalid-write-rejected, :rejection-leaves-state-unchanged} against a real Mongo connection — so per-op invalid-write rejection AND per-op valid writes ARE integration-tested against Mongo for all seven ops, directly refuting both the 'repository-location-only' claim and the 'four ops have no Mongo write test at all' claim. A misregistered op keyword would fail law-valid-write-accepted for that op in this very suite. The card's verification row ('All five ops validate — Per-op invalid-write rejection against real Mongo', conservative since seven ops are covered) accurately describes what the tests prove."]}
  {:title "ep inbox decide --repo is now a silent no-op flag",
   :file "src/epiphany/infra/main.clj",
   :detail
   "`inbox-decide-options` still includes `repo-option` (committed line 1047), but the rewritten `run-inbox-decide` never reads `(:repo options)` — the old code used it via `repository-identity/resolve-repository` to pick the recording resource-id. Recording under the candidate's own resource-id is the documented behavior change, but leaving a parsed, advertised flag that does nothing is a silent-ignore; it should be removed from the options (or rejected) as part of the same change.",
   :severity "nit",
   :card "eng-017g2",
   :lens "correctness",
   :survives false,
   :reasons
   ["The factual observation is accurate — inbox-decide-options still includes repo-option (line 1033) and run-inbox-decide never reads (:repo options), because the rewired seam deliberately records decisions under the candidate's own resource-id (a behavior change the card's IMPLEMENTED comment documents explicitly at line 111). But the finding fails the promotion-gating test on two grounds: (1) The claimed severity is 'nit', and a leftover-but-harmless parsed flag is exactly the class of issue that should not gate review→done. The flag is validated and accepted, produces no wrong behavior, and the new semantics are documented. (2) Retaining --repo is plausibly the correct compatibility choice, not an oversight: removing it would make every pre-existing script that passes `--repo <path>` fail with an 'unknown option' parse error, whereas keeping it lets old invocations continue to parse while the new (documented) recording semantics apply. A silent-ignore that preserves backward compatibility for a deliberately changed semantic is a defensible tradeoff; if it bothers anyone it is a one-line cleanup for the CLI-hygiene pass, not a defect in this card's delivered scope. The card's acceptance criteria (shared decoder seam, parity tests, green suite — all evidenced) are unaffected."
    "The factual core is accurate — inbox-decide-options (main.clj:1033) still lists repo-option and run-inbox-decide (1107–1149) never reads (:repo options), since the card deliberately changed recording to target the candidate's own resource-id (card line 111 documents exactly this behavior change). But the finding is self-classified as a nit and does not gate a review→done promotion: (1) the flag being retained means existing scripts/invocations that pass --repo continue to parse successfully rather than hard-erroring — removing it is a breaking CLI surface change, while keeping it parsed is the conservative choice during a seam transition; (2) the card's delivered scope is the decode/execute/encode seam and parity tests, all of which pass (707 tests, 0 failures, parity extended to review-decisions); the help text for a single-surface legacy flag is cosmetic cleanup that any follow-up CLI card (004A/B/D, 005B/F, which own their own wiring into this seam per the card's Dependencies section) can absorb; (3) the behavior change itself is documented on the card and in the run-inbox-decide docstring, so this is not a hidden divergence — it is an advertised legacy flag awaiting deprecation, not a correctness, parity, or contract violation. A nit-level cosmetic cleanup does not block promotion."]}
  {:title
   "validated-insert! treats ANY duplicate-key as a stable replay (returns nil) with no content comparison — the same 'different record masquerading as replay' class the commit fixed for repository-location, left open on the other six write ops",
   :file "src/epiphany/infra/adapters/mongo.clj",
   :detail
   "For :record-ingestion-run!, :record-checkpoint!, :record-section-extraction!, :record-revision-at-path!, :record-review-decision!, :record-lineage-candidate!, the dup-key branch (mongo.clj:608-613) returns nil unconditionally on error code 11000. A retry carrying the same :observation/id (or same request-id for review-decision/lineage-candidate, which have unique request_id indexes, mongo.clj:118-134) with materially different content is silently swallowed as 'stable' — exactly what the commit's own docstring at mongo.clj:566-572 says must not happen, and which insert-or-idempotent! detects via full-map decode equality for repository-location. State parity with the reference adapter also diverges here: for the four ops without request-id indexes, in-memory appends a duplicate (in_memory.clj:129-143) so export-all content differs between adapters after an identical replay; for review-decision/lineage-candidate both adapters silently ignore changed content (in_memory.clj:147-166), so the hole is at least consistent — but inconsistent with the conflict semantics this card establishes as the norm. The ENG-017D laws only cover :record-repository-location!, so the suite cannot catch any of this.",
   :severity "should-fix",
   :card "eng-017e",
   :lens "correctness",
   :survives false,
   :reasons
   ["The finding's central factual premise is false: it claims 'the ENG-017D laws only cover :record-repository-location!, so the suite cannot catch any of this.' In reality, test/epiphany/law_suite/observations_laws.clj judges all seven write ops with the universal laws, and its op-fixtures declare :idempotency :first-write-wins with make-conflict generators for BOTH :record-review-decision! (changes :review-decision/decision to :rejected) and :record-lineage-candidate! (changes confidence) — the changed-content-replay law runs against Mongo for these ops and observations_mongo_test.clj:81-87 explicitly asserts :pass for changed-content-replay on all three request-id-bearing ops. The laws ns docstring states 'the harness is no longer repository-location-only.' Second, the behavior the claim calls a 'hole' for review-decision/lineage-candidate is the deliberate, documented, law-tested contract: ':first-write-wins — any replay returns nil; the stored fact stays the original' (observations_laws.clj:19-20, 175-183), matching the in-memory adapter's documented 'retries do not duplicate' semantics (in_memory.clj:144-166). The claim even admits adapter consistency here but mischaracterizes the card as establishing :full conflict semantics 'as the norm' — the card and law fixtures deliberately assign :full only to repository-location. Third, the mongo.clj:566-572 docstring is scoped to doc->observation-equal? (used only by insert-or-idempotent! for repository-location); validated-insert! carries its own documenting comment that a dup-key on an append-only record is a stable replay (mongo.clj:608-611). Fourth, this dup-key->nil handling for the other ops is grandfathered: it exists identically in 91ab60f^ (11000 checks at old lines 610/622), so it is not something this card introduced, and the card's Non-goals state 'existing encoding stays unless a law failure proves it wrong' — no law fails. The residual export-all divergence for the four :idempotency :none ops is a fixture-declared non-goal ('no request-id idempotency laws apply') and outside the card's differential invariant, which is on law outcome categories and passes. Real-seeming but factually wrong about coverage, and the substance is deliberate, documented, and tested semantics — not a promotion-gating defect."
    "The code mechanics are accurately described, but the finding fails scope and provenance tests. (1) Grandfathered: for :record-review-decision! and :record-lineage-candidate!, the 11000→nil swallow predates this card (git show 91ab60f~1 shows the identical catch-and-nil with 'Duplicate request_id — idempotent replay, not an error'), and the in-memory reference adapter has always done exactly the same thing (request-id index check returns nil without content comparison, in_memory.clj:147-166). A card that adds write-validation cannot be gated on not redesigning a pre-existing, reference-adapter-matching idempotency semantic the ENG-017D laws never covered. (2) For the four append-only ops, the masquerade scenario the commit fixed is structurally inapplicable: every doc's _id is the :observation/id (mongo.clj:236/298/345/411 etc.), and those collections have no request-id unique index, so a dup key can only mean the SAME observation identity was resubmitted — not a different record under a shared request-id, which was the repository-location hazard (unique request_id index letting a new :observation/id masquerade). Identity collision under append-only observation semantics is a producer contract violation, not a replay-ambiguity the adapter can adjudicate; arguably Mongo's dedupe-by-identity is more correct than the in-memory blind append. Pre-card, these four ops let the raw driver exception escape; the card strictly improved them toward graceful replay. (3) Scope: the card's differential invariant is explicitly 'In-memory and Mongo agree on every SHARED LAW's acceptance/rejection category,' and by the finding's own admission the ENG-017D laws only cover :record-repository-location! — the one op where full-map decode equality and :idempotency-conflict were delivered and law-proven. Extending content-comparison idempotency to six ops with no shared laws, no acceptance criterion, and no ADR-004 mandate is new scope (belongs to a future laws card), not a defect in this delivery. Real observation, wrong card, not promotion-gating."]}
  {:title
   "validated-insert! swallows every duplicate-key as a 'stable replay' with no content comparison — the same masquerade defect the commit fixed for repository-location survives on the other six write ops",
   :file "src/epiphany/infra/adapters/mongo.clj",
   :detail
   "insert-or-idempotent! (lines 587-598) was fixed so a duplicate key triggers a full-map decode equality check and returns {:code :idempotency-conflict} on materially different content. But validated-insert! (lines 601-616), used by :record-ingestion-run!, :record-checkpoint!, :record-section-extraction!, :record-revision-at-path!, :record-review-decision!, :record-lineage-candidate!, returns nil on ANY MongoWriteException code 11000 with no content check — a different record reusing the same _id (observation/id) is silently swallowed as a replay, exactly the bug class the commit message says it eliminated. There is also an adapter divergence here: the in-memory reference APPENDS duplicates unconditionally for ingestion-run/checkpoint/section-extraction/revision-at-path (in_memory.clj:124-143), so the same same-_id replay is a no-op in Mongo but a duplicated record in-memory (observable via :export-all). The card's differential invariant is only per-law and the laws only exercise idempotency for repository-location, so the acceptance criteria are technically met — but the parity claim in the commit message is narrower than it reads, and the law suite currently cannot catch this.",
   :severity "should-fix",
   :card "eng-017e",
   :lens "dod-coverage",
   :survives false,
   :reasons
   ["The code citations are accurate but the finding's framing and gating force fail on inspection. (1) The claim is wrong for two of the six ops: the shared ENG-017D law fixture (observations_laws.clj:160-183) explicitly declares :first-write-wins idempotency for :record-review-decision! and :record-lineage-candidate!, and law-changed-content-replay (lines 256-259) REQUIRES that a changed-content replay return nil, not :idempotency-conflict. Mongo's validated-insert! returning nil on dup-key for those ops is exactly law-conformant behavior — the 'masquerade defect' framing is inverted there, and the claim's statement that 'the laws only exercise idempotency for repository-location' is false. (2) For the remaining four ops the same fixture deliberately declares :idempotency :none — no idempotency semantics are part of the shared contract, and the card's differential invariant is explicitly scoped to 'every shared law's acceptance/rejection category' (card Invariants). The Mongo/in-memory divergence (silent dedup on _id vs unconditional append) is therefore outside the delivered scope; the in-memory append behavior is grandfathered (in_memory.clj:89-95 documents these as 'non-idempotent record ops', predating commit 91ab60f). (3) The defect scenario requires a caller reusing :observation/id (the Mongo _id) — a primary-key collision, not a request-id replay; these record kinds' fixtures largely carry no request-id at all (they dissoc it), and observation ids are minted via random-uuid in domain code, so the specific bug class the commit fixed (same request-id, different observation/id) is not reachable through validated-insert!. (4) The card's scope required that driver exceptions not escape the adapter; before this commit a duplicate _id on those four collections threw a raw MongoWriteException, and the card converted that to a documented nil-on-dup-key normalization with an explicit comment ('an exact replay of an already-recorded observation is stable, not an error'). The claim itself concedes the acceptance criteria are met and rates itself should-fix. The residual — extending the law suite to cover _id-collision semantics or aligning the four :none adapters — is a legitimate follow-up card, not a gate on this review→done promotion."
    "The code behavior is described accurately, but the finding is contract-deliberate, not a defect. The ENG-017D law suite (a done dependency this card must satisfy, test/epiphany/law_suite/observations_laws.clj:160-183) explicitly assigns idempotency semantics per record kind: repository-location is :full (the only kind where changed-content replay must yield :idempotency-conflict), review-decision and lineage-candidate are :first-write-wins whose documented semantics are 'any replay returns nil; the stored fact stays' — so Mongo's validated-insert! returning nil on duplicate key for those two ops is exactly the contracted behavior, not a masquerade. The remaining four kinds (ingestion-run, checkpoint, section-extraction, revision-at-path) are declared :idempotency :none, meaning the shared contract deliberately defines no replay semantics for append-only event records; the card's differential invariant is explicitly 'in-memory and Mongo agree on every shared law's acceptance/rejection category,' and there is no shared law governing same-_id replays for :none kinds, so the Mongo-vs-in-memory divergence on :export-all lives outside the contract both adapters were aligned to. The commit comment's parity claim is scoped precisely: 'Idempotency equality is full-map decode equality... exactly the case the ENG-017D conflict law exercises' — that law only runs for :full kinds, i.e., repository-location, so the claim that the commit message promises bug-class elimination across all six ops overreads it. The exposure scenario (same :observation/id UUID reused with materially different content for a :none-kind record) is a caller bug the contract intentionally leaves undefined, and the reviewer concedes the acceptance criteria are technically met. Real nuance worth a follow-up card (e.g., extending the law framework), but not a promotion-gating defect for this card."]}
  {:title
   "Card verification row claims 'Per-op invalid-write rejection against real Mongo' but the cited ENG-017D suite only exercises one op",
   :file
   "docs/kanban/stories/engineering-assurance-mongo-contract-alignment.md",
   :detail
   "The card's Verification table maps 'All five ops validate' to evidence 'Per-op invalid-write rejection against real Mongo — ENG-017D suite via clojure -M:integration-test'. The ENG-017D law suite (test/epiphany/law_suite/observations_laws.clj:76-150) only ever invokes (:record-repository-location! port); no law sends an invalid record to :record-ingestion-run!, :record-checkpoint!, :record-section-extraction!, :record-revision-at-path!, :record-review-decision!, or :record-lineage-candidate!. Validation IS wired for all seven ops (validated-insert! calls validate-write!, and valid writes through those paths are exercised by the pre-existing integration suite — verified: clojure -M:integration-test => 17 tests, 53 assertions, only the 2 pre-existing integration_suite_test.clj failures), but the per-op invalid-write rejection evidence the card claims does not exist. Under ADR-004 decision 7 (generated evidence authoritative over completion claims) the completion comment overstates what was demonstrated; either the laws need per-op invalid fixtures or the claim should be scoped to what ran.",
   :severity "nit",
   :card "eng-017e",
   :lens "architecture-law",
   :survives false,
   :reasons
   ["The claim misreads the harness. observations_laws.clj:205-227 defines law-invalid-write-rejected and law-rejection-leaves-state-unchanged as parameterized over `op`, calling ((get port op) (invalid-record)) — and the runner (observations-laws, lines 341-348) iterates ALL judged ops (default: every key in op-fixtures, all seven write ops) against the universal laws. Only the export/import round-trip law is pinned to :record-repository-location! (line 298), which is by design a global law, not a per-op invalid-write law. The Mongo test (test/epiphany/law_suite/observations_mongo_test.clj) declares all capabilities and explicitly asserts per-op that [:invalid-write-rejected] and [:rejection-leaves-state-unchanged] pass for every op in laws/op-fixtures, i.e. per-op invalid-write rejection IS exercised against real Mongo for all seven ops. The Verification row's claim ('Per-op invalid-write rejection against real Mongo — ENG-017D suite') is therefore accurate, and the reviewer's cited line range (76-150) only covers fixture builders, not the law definitions or runner that refute their reading."
    "The claim is factually wrong — it misreads the law suite's structure. The reviewer cites test/epiphany/law_suite/observations_laws.clj:76-150 and concludes invalid writes are only sent to :record-repository-location!, but lines 76-150 are just the valid-record fixture builders. The actual invalid-write laws are universal: law-invalid-write-rejected (lines 205-215) calls ((get port op) (invalid-record)) for whatever op it is invoked with, and the runner (lines 341-348) applies every universal law — including :invalid-write-rejected and :rejection-leaves-state-unchanged — to every op in judged-ops, which defaults to (keys op-fixtures): all seven write operations (:record-repository-location!, :record-revision-at-path!, :record-ingestion-run!, :record-checkpoint!, :record-section-extraction!, :record-review-decision!, :record-lineage-candidate!). The ns docstring (lines 10-13) states this explicitly: 'every registered write operation is judged by the same universal laws (valid accepted, invalid rejected, rejection-without-mutation)... the harness is no longer repository-location-only.' Only the export-import-round-trip global law (line 296-298) is pinned to repository-location, by design. So per-op invalid-write rejection against real Mongo is exactly what the Mongo run of this suite (observations_mongo_test.clj, capabilities all declared, no :skip outcomes possible for :schema-validation) exercises — seven ops x two rejection laws. The card's verification row accurately describes delivered evidence; the completion comment's 'all six laws pass' matches the six defined laws. The finding rests on a reading of the fixture section as the law section and does not survive contact with the runner code."]}
  {:title
   "Executor catch-all re-labels service/internal faults as :rejected (400) and discards structured ex-data",
   :file "src/epiphany/application/commands.clj",
   :detail
   "execute-register (lines 90-93) and execute-search (lines 103-106) map every ExceptionInfo whose :code is not :unavailable to :rejected — the category the same file's docstring (line 16) defines as 'client input failed validation'. Concrete failing scenario: the Ollama adapter throws ex-info with {:status ... :body ...} and NO :code on a non-200 response (infra/adapters/ollama.clj:36-38); a semantic/hybrid search that gets a 500 from a live-but-broken Ollama therefore returns HTTP 400 'Ollama embed failed: 500' / CLI 'Error: ...' blaming the client, instead of UNAVAILABLE — contrary to AGENTS.md's UNAVAILABLE semantics and STYLE.md's rule against substituting a wrong category for an explicit unavailable/invalid result. Same path turns internal port-contract breaches (e.g. in_memory.clj:55 {:code :schema-validation-failed}) into client-facing 400s with raw internal messages, re-opening for search the leak ENG-017G's wrap-exceptions closed (register's echo is a documented ENG-017G deferral, so this finding is scoped to search + the category mislabel). Same catch also drops ex-data: main.clj:125-134's outer handler that used to print 'Git error:'/:hint detail for registration failures is now unreachable for register!-thrown ex-infos, so CLI register loses boundary context it previously showed (STYLE.md foreign-boundary rule step 2: capture meaningful failure context).",
   :severity "should-fix",
   :card "eng-017g2",
   :lens "architecture-law",
   :survives false,
   :reasons
   ["The claim survives on the facts. Verified directly: commands.clj:90-93 and 103-106 catch every ExceptionInfo whose ex-data :code is not :unavailable and map it to :rejected, which the same file's docstring (line 16) defines as 'client input failed validation' and http-status-for-category maps to 400. ollama.clj:36-38 confirms the adapter throws ex-info with only {:status :body} and no :code on non-200, so a semantic/hybrid search against a live-but-erroring Ollama (e.g. model not pulled -> 404, embed 500) passes the TCP probe and is then reported as HTTP 400 'Bad Request' / CLI 'Error: Ollama embed failed: 500', blaming valid client input. This is not grandfathered code: git history shows application/commands.clj was created wholesale by this card's own commit 7977f2a, and the executor's normalized outcome categories are the card's core deliverable per its Scope section ('one source of truth, not two parallel maps'), so the miscategorization is squarely in scope. The card's comment history does not document this as an accepted tradeoff — the documented deferral is register-handler's raw-ExceptionInfo echo (a leak fix owned elsewhere), not the category mislabel. The ex-data-drop sub-claim is also verified by diff: pre-card run-register's outer catch (still present at main.clj:125-134) printed 'Git error:'/:hint detail for register!-thrown ex-infos like 'Not a Git repository'; post-card, execute-register's catch intercepts those first and flattens them to the bare message, a real CLI diagnostic regression against STYLE.md's foreign-boundary rule. Whether :unavailable is the philosophically perfect category is debatable, but labeling a server-side service fault as client-input :rejected contradicts the seam's own category contract that this card exists to enforce, and no test pins that catch-all behavior as intended. Real, in-scope, card-delivered, and user-facing on both surfaces."
    "Verified the code: the claim's mechanics are accurate (execute-register/execute-search at commands.clj:90-93,103-106 do map any ExceptionInfo lacking :code :unavailable to :rejected; ollama.clj:36-38 throws ex-info with only :status/:body; main.clj:125-134's ex-data detail printer is now unreachable for exceptions the executor swallows). But the finding does not survive scope and gating analysis. (1) Everything it says about the register path is explicitly out of scope: the card's Non-goals section states 'Not fixing register-handler's raw-ExceptionInfo echo ... both flagged on ENG-017G for their own cards', and the claim itself concedes register's echo is a documented deferral — the ex-data/Git-context loss argument is also register-centric and therefore equally deferred. (2) The search-path residue is a fault-taxonomy edge, not a parity or contract violation: AGENTS.md's UNAVAILABLE semantics mandate 'an unreachable selected service is UNAVAILABLE' — that exact case is deliberately handled by the :service-available? probe the card added (recorded in the implementation comment, tested on both surfaces). A TCP-reachable-but-500ing Ollama is outside that documented semantics, and the card's own acceptance criteria (parity by construction, decode table tests, green suite) are met — both surfaces return the identical outcome in this scenario, so the card's core guarantee holds. (3) Severity is claimed as should-fix, and the scenario (live Ollama returning non-200 mid-search) is a rare operational edge; reclassifying adapter-internal faults into a finer category set is a legitimate follow-up card, not a gate on review→done for a card whose deliverable was the decode/execute/encode seam and parity extension. Default-to-refute applies: real but out-of-scope (register) or non-gating minor (search)."]}
  {:title
   "run-search lost all exception handling — raw adapter exceptions now crash the CLI with a stack trace",
   :file "src/epiphany/infra/main.clj",
   :detail
   "In 7977f2a the rewritten `run-search` (committed main.clj ~lines 366-415) calls `(commands/execute ...)` with no try/catch, and neither `run` (~line 1254) nor `-main` catches anything. The removed code shows the old `run-search` had `(catch clojure.lang.ExceptionInfo e ...)` and `(catch Exception e {:exit 1 :out ...})`. `execute-search` in application/commands.clj:101-106 only catches ExceptionInfo, so raw exceptions escape: (a) probe→call TOCTOU — `ollama/available?` passes, then `embed-request`'s `(.send client ...)` (infra/adapters/ollama.clj:34) throws java.net.ConnectException when Ollama dies in between; (b) a corrupt/unreadable Lucene index makes the search ports throw raw IOException. Both now produce an uncaught JVM stack trace and no clean `{:exit 1 :out \"Error: ...\"}`. HTTP handles the same failure via wrap-exceptions (500 problem), so this is also a CLI-vs-HTTP error-path divergence the seam was meant to eliminate.",
   :severity "should-fix",
   :card "eng-017g2",
   :lens "correctness",
   :survives true,
   :reasons
   ["Verified against the code: the claim is factually accurate. (1) Current run-search (main.clj:367-416) calls commands/execute with no try/catch; run (1240-1281) and -main (1283-1286) catch nothing. (2) The pre-card run-search did have `(catch Exception e {:exit 1 :out ...})` — visible as a removed line in 7977f2a's diff — so this is a behavior regression introduced by this card's own rewrite, not grandfathered code. (3) execute-search (application/commands.clj:101-106) catches only ExceptionInfo; a java.net.ConnectException from the probe→call TOCTOU window (ollama/available? passes, then .send throws) or a raw IOException from a corrupt Lucene index escapes the seam entirely, producing a JVM stack trace instead of the clean `{:exit 1 :out \"Error: ...\"}` the CLI previously returned. (4) HTTP's wrap-exceptions (http.clj:156,179) catches all Exceptions into a 500 problem, so CLI and HTTP now diverge on exactly the error-path parity the seam exists to guarantee — raw exceptions bypass the shared encoder, which is a structural hole in the card's core invariant (normalized outcome categories as single source of truth), not an orthogonal concern. (5) The card's IMPLEMENTED comment documents other accepted behavior changes but never mentions dropping the catch-all, so it is not a recorded tradeoff. Severity 'should-fix' is fair: it regresses an existing user-facing failure path in code this card rewrote. Finding survives."
    "Verified against the code at 7977f2a: the claim's facts are accurate and the regression is real and in-scope. (1) src/epiphany/infra/main.clj:367-416 — the rewritten run-search calls (commands/execute ...) with no try/catch; run (line 1240) and -main (line 1283) also catch nothing, so a raw exception propagates to an uncaught JVM stack trace. (2) The diff shows the old run-search had both (catch clojure.lang.ExceptionInfo e ...) and a generic (catch Exception e {:exit 1 :out ...}) — this card's commit removed the generic catch. (3) application/commands.clj:95-106 (execute-search) catches only ExceptionInfo; infra/adapters/ollama.clj:34 (.send client ...) throws java.net.ConnectException/IOException when Ollama dies between the :service-available? probe and the embed call (genuine TOCTOU, not theoretical — the probe at line 109-120 is a separate TCP connect), and a corrupt Lucene index throws raw IOException deterministically. (4) HTTP's wrap-exceptions (infra/http.clj:179-181) catches bare Exception and returns a 500 problem, so CLI now crashes while HTTP degrades cleanly — exactly the error-path divergence this seam card ('parity guaranteed by construction', normalized outcome categories) exists to eliminate. Nothing in the card's comment history documents dropping the generic catch as an accepted tradeoff — the IMPLEMENTED comment only covers the probe-based UNAVAILABLE parity, which does not address the post-probe failure window or the Lucene IOException case. This is a behavior regression introduced by the card itself (not grandfathered code), on a command the card explicitly rewired, and the fix is a small generic catch in execute-search or run-search. It legitimately gates review→done."]}]}
